 "instructions": "You translate one page of Alexander Grothendieck's handwritten mathematical notes (Montpellier archive) from French\ninto English. The page is a LaTeX transcription of the manuscript. Some passages are already in English (Grothendieck\nwrote parts of these notes in English); keep those exactly as they are.\n\nWrite the English version of the whole page, as a faithful raw translation for mathematicians: close to the\nwording, keeping Grothendieck's abbreviations, telegraphic style, question marks and hesitations. Do not smooth,\ncomplete, correct or explain anything.\n\nKeep every piece of LaTeX exactly as it is:\n- every macro and its arguments' structure: \\scanpage{...}, \\mspage{...}, \\margin{...}, \\deleted{...},\n  \\inserted{...}, \\unsure{...}, \\illegible{...}, \\uline{...}, \\diagram{...}, environments, line breaks of displays;\n- every formula, symbol and diagram: change no mathematics at all, not even an obvious slip;\n- \\scanpage and \\mspage lines unchanged, in their places.\nTranslate the French words inside \\deleted, \\inserted, \\margin and \\unsure too. An \\unsure{a|b} with alternative\nreadings: translate each alternative and keep the | between them (if two French alternatives give the same English,\nstill keep both). French words inside \\text{...} or \\mathrm{...} in formulas: translate them only when they are\nordinary words (e.g. \\text{si} -> \\text{if}); keep names of objects and notation as they are.\nMathematical terms: use the standard English terms (champ -> stack, cat\u00e9gorie fibr\u00e9e -> fibred category,\ntopos, site, faisceau -> sheaf, gerbe -> gerbe, fibration, cofibration, \u00e9quivalence faible -> weak equivalence,\nmod\u00e8le ferm\u00e9 -> closed model, etc.).\n\nAnswer in exactly this form, with nothing before the first marker:\n=====BEGIN TARGET_NEW=====\n(the complete English page)\n=====END TARGET_NEW=====\n=====NOTES=====\n(readings you were unsure how to translate, with the French; or \"none\")\n"